{"sequence": 53, "event": "completed", "config_version": 11, "result": {"answer": "The evidence identifies multiple instances where Art Whaley and Julie Kramer fought or disagreed about school-related matters, spanning from March 2023 through July 2026. The most direct and explicitly labeled fight occurred on March 28, 2023, when Art stated 'I honestly don't know what you want to fight about, today' and Julie responded 'I don't want to fight about anything.' This exchange was triggered by school-related issues: Julie cited school days leaving her almost no time with Olivia, school tuition as part of her financial burden, and disagreements about school break custody time. The fight then transitioned into a substantive disagreement about Olivia's school (Wildflower), its reading curriculum, and whether Olivia was being pushed enough academically.\n\nAdditional school-related disagreements occurred on:\n- November 13, 2023: Art and Julie disagreed over whether to continue the microschool vs. homeschooling at home, with Art citing 'sunk cost fallacy' and Julie insisting the setup was complete and stable.\n- June 26, 2024: Art questioned a reduced 2.5-day school schedule, saying '4 days a week of individual instruction was better than 5 of a classroom.' Julie defended the arrangement, saying fluff time was being cut, not academics.\n- July 10, 2024: Art raised concern about Olivia being in 'not real school' and needing group participation skills; Julie pushed back, calling 120 kids at theater camp 'a big jump.'\n- July 16, 2025: A sustained argument where Art said Olivia should be 'OUT of gymnastics until her education is on track,' claimed she did zero school for two-week stretches at his house, and said 'I'm not trying to pick a fight about it.' Julie countered that Olivia was on track, that the farm schoolwork was 'way too easy, like pre-school stuff,' and defended homeschooling.\n- August 4, 2025: A tense exchange where Art implied Julie was not doing enough schoolwork ('If she's only doing school work here she needs to get here as often as possible'), and Julie pushed back saying Olivia does school 'almost at least 3-4 days a week.'\n- July 1, 2026: A prolonged argument where Art accused Julie of 'neglecting her education again' and demanded 'final say about activities and education' in any settlement. Julie strongly disputed being called neglectful, cited her paid tuition and hours spent on curriculum, and criticized Art's 'Time for Learning' online curriculum as poorly set up.\n\nSeveral other school-related discussions (June 12, 2023; July 25, 2023; September 22, 2023; September 29, 2023) were collaborative rather than fights, involving school options, school drama with third parties, and problem-solving. The February 23, 2024 argument was primarily about gymnastics competition logistics and parental time, not school per se, though it touched on broader priorities.\n\nThe question 'When did we fight about school?' is ambiguous as to which specific fight it refers to, and the term 'fight' is subjective — some exchanges show tension or disagreement that may or may not meet the threshold of a 'fight' depending on interpretation. The March 28, 2023 exchange is the only one where a party explicitly used the word 'fight' in connection with school-related topics.", "answer_summary": "Art and Julie fought about school on multiple dates: March 28, 2023 (the most explicit, with Art saying 'I don't know what you want to fight about'), November 13, 2023, June 26, 2024, July 10, 2024, July 16, 2025, August 4, 2025, and July 1, 2026. The March 28, 2023 exchange is the only one where a party explicitly used the word 'fight' in a school-related context.", "strategy": "windowed_ledger", "evidence_ledger": [{"range_id": "r000001", "thread_id": "julie_kramer", "start_message_id": "decipher_message_1:3572", "end_message_id": "decipher_message_1:3556", "summary": "Julie and Art argue about custody time during Olivia's two-week spring break. Julie insists on keeping Olivia for the full week rather than sending her to the farm for two weeks, citing that school days leave her almost no time with Olivia and that she bears 100% of financial responsibility. Art counters that many dads have 50/50 custody, that Julie's financial pressure isn't solely from parenting, and that Olivia herself wanted two weeks at the farm. Both express frustration about last-minute schedule changes and not seeing Olivia enough.", "relevance": "This passage contains the most direct evidence of a 'fight about school' — the argument is explicitly triggered by how school days limit Julie's time with Olivia, Julie's financial burden including school tuition, disagreements about school breaks, and the strain the school schedule places on co-parenting. The dispute encompasses school-related topics (tuition, school schedule, school breaks, reading curriculum) and constitutes a fight between the parties.", "disposition": "used", "rationale": "Direct evidence of a fight about school on March 28, 2023: Julie cites school days limiting her time with Olivia, school tuition as financial burden, and school break custody disputes. Art and Julie express frustration about the school schedule's strain on co-parenting."}, {"range_id": "r000002", "thread_id": "julie_kramer", "start_message_id": "decipher_message_1:3555", "end_message_id": "decipher_message_1:3548", "summary": "The argument continues with Julie detailing $3500+ in medical bills, $6600 max out-of-pocket, monthly insurance premiums, and school tuition as costs she alone bears. Art responds that moms can pay child support too, that Julie's spending choices (new truck, Edmond address, cosmetic surgeries) contribute to financial pressure, and says 'I honestly don't know what you want to fight about, today.' Julie replies she doesn't want to fight and always wants to co-parent peacefully.", "relevance": "Art explicitly labels the exchange as a fight ('I honestly don't know what you want to fight about, today'), and the dispute is intertwined with school tuition and school-related financial obligations. Julie's reference to school tuition as part of her financial burden directly connects the fight to school costs.", "disposition": "used", "rationale": "Art explicitly labels the exchange as a fight ('I honestly don't know what you want to fight about, today') on March 28, 2023, with school tuition directly cited by Julie as part of her financial burden. Julie responds 'I don't want to fight about anything.'"}, {"range_id": "r000003", "thread_id": "julie_kramer", "start_message_id": "decipher_message_1:3544", "end_message_id": "decipher_message_1:3516", "summary": "The conversation transitions from the custody/financial fight into a discussion about Olivia's school. Julie mentions signing a commitment letter for Wildflower school, concerns about public school (Deer Creek) quality, and both parents discuss the school's approach to reading and math. Art wishes there was more pressure on core skills like reading and math, while Julie notes Miss Chris wanted to move Olivia into the reading curriculum but Olivia refused.", "relevance": "This passage shows the fight de-escalating into a substantive disagreement about Olivia's school — specifically the school's educational philosophy, reading curriculum, and whether Olivia is being pushed enough academically. This is a direct discussion about school that emerges from the broader fight.", "disposition": "used", "rationale": "The March 28, 2023 fight de-escalates into a substantive disagreement about Olivia's school — Wildflower's educational philosophy, reading curriculum, and whether Olivia is being pushed enough academically. Art wishes 'more pressure on reading and math.'"}, {"range_id": "r000004", "thread_id": "julie_kramer", "start_message_id": "decipher_message_1:3023", "end_message_id": "decipher_message_1:3022", "summary": "Art says sailing around the world is 'Still safer than public schools in America now,' and Julie responds 'which is why she's in private.' This exchange references school but is not a fight about school.", "relevance": "Mentions school (public vs private) but does not show a fight or argument about school between the parties.", "disposition": "not_material", "rationale": "Art and Julie mention public vs. private school on June 12, 2023, but this is a brief two-message exchange with no fight or argument about school between the parties."}, {"range_id": "r000005", "thread_id": "julie_kramer", "start_message_id": "decipher_message_1:2655", "end_message_id": "decipher_message_1:2618", "summary": "Julie and Art discuss Wildflower school being 'up in flames,' parents upset about new owners (the Heinigers) not paying teachers, Miss Chris possibly leaving to start her own school, and Julie's desire for Olivia to follow Miss Chris. They discuss Keystone waitlist, Acton Academy, and forming a new school. The tone is collaborative, not a fight between Julie and Art about school.", "relevance": "Extensive discussion about Olivia's school situation, but it is a collaborative discussion between Julie and Art about school options, not a fight between them about school.", "disposition": "not_material", "rationale": "Extensive July 25, 2023 discussion about Wildflower school instability and alternatives, but tone is collaborative between Julie and Art, not a fight between them about school."}, {"range_id": "r000006", "thread_id": "julie_kramer", "start_message_id": "decipher_message_1:2139", "end_message_id": "decipher_message_1:2056", "summary": "Julie describes drama with Jami (who owned the school LLC), Jami's kids bullying, parents wanting more academics/reading, and the school dissolving. Julie lists non-negotiable education requirements for Olivia. Art agrees and offers to homeschool. This is a collaborative discussion about school problems with a third party, not a fight between Julie and Art.", "relevance": "Shows school-related conflict with Jami (a third party), not a fight between Julie and Art about school.", "disposition": "not_material", "rationale": "September 22, 2023 discussion about school drama with Jami (a third party), not a fight between Julie and Art about school. Art agrees with Julie and offers to homeschool."}, {"range_id": "r000007", "thread_id": "julie_kramer", "start_message_id": "decipher_message_1:1851", "end_message_id": "decipher_message_1:1733", "summary": "Julie announces she 'fired Chris & Emily' (the teachers), says they were not qualified and didn't teach reading. Julie and Art discuss multiple school options (Keystone, Westminster, Casady, Epic, Back to Earth, Dove Science Academy, public schools, homeschooling). Art suggests Epic Charter, Deer Creek, or homeschooling at the farm. They debate Back to Earth being too hippie, public school class sizes (28 kids per teacher), and Julie's insistence on reading instruction. While there is some tension about options, the overall tone is collaborative problem-solving rather than a fight between them.", "relevance": "Shows extensive discussion and some disagreement about school options and approaches, but primarily collaborative problem-solving rather than a fight between Julie and Art about school.", "disposition": "not_material", "rationale": "September 29, 2023 discussion about school options after firing teachers shows some tension about options but is primarily collaborative problem-solving, not a fight between Julie and Art about school."}, {"range_id": "r000008", "thread_id": "julie_kramer", "start_message_id": "decipher_message_1:986", "end_message_id": "decipher_message_1:972", "summary": "Art suggests homeschooling at home as an alternative to the microschool, citing sunk cost fallacy and instability; Julie pushes back, saying the school is set up and stable and Olivia would hate homeschooling. Art continues advocating for stability and suggests homeschool can work if needed; Julie insists the hard part is done and the schedule won't change. This is a disagreement about school arrangements.", "relevance": "Directly relevant to the question of fighting about school: Art and Julie disagree over whether to continue the microschool vs. homeschooling at home, with Art repeatedly suggesting homeschool and Julie rejecting it.", "disposition": "used", "rationale": "November 13, 2023 disagreement about school arrangements: Art repeatedly suggests homeschooling at home as an alternative to the microschool, citing 'sunk cost fallacy' and instability; Julie rejects it, insisting the setup is complete and Olivia would hate it."}, {"range_id": "r000009", "thread_id": "julie_kramer", "start_message_id": "decipher_message_1:976", "end_message_id": "decipher_message_1:976", "summary": "Art says 'It's a sunk cost fallacy at some point' and 'she can't just keep having it shaken up,' while saying he's not telling her to give up now, just not to chase it if things keep going wrong. Julie responds that the hard and expensive part was getting set up and that's done.", "relevance": "Continuation of the school disagreement: Art raises concerns about the school's viability and Julie counters that the setup is complete.", "disposition": "redundant", "rationale": "Single message from r000008 already captured in that range; Art's 'sunk cost fallacy' statement is duplicated evidence from the same November 13, 2023 exchange."}, {"range_id": "r000010", "thread_id": "julie_kramer", "start_message_id": "decipher_message_1:128", "end_message_id": "decipher_message_1:110", "summary": "A heated argument erupts over whether Olivia should come home the night before a gymnastics competition so Julie can do her competition hair, or stay at the farm/Grace's house. Art says 'you think her hairdo is more important than time with her father' and argues gymnastics is 'about you,' not Olivia. Julie responds by detailing the financial investment in gymnastics ($761/month plus fees totaling over $11,000/year) and says 'You don't help pay for anything.' Art says 'time with her family is what is most important' and references Julie's own gymnastics abuse history. Julie clarifies she was saying she should have kept Olivia because of logistics, not because gymnastics is more important than family.", "relevance": "This is a direct fight involving school/gymnastics priorities and parental time, with both parties expressing hurt and disagreement over priorities. While primarily about gymnastics competition logistics, it touches on the broader conflict about activities vs. family time.", "disposition": "not_material", "rationale": "February 23, 2024 heated argument is primarily about gymnastics competition logistics and parental time, not school. While it touches on broader priorities, it does not constitute a fight about school between the parties."}, {"range_id": "r000011", "thread_id": "julie_kramer", "start_message_id": "decipher_export_19:583", "end_message_id": "decipher_export_19:603", "summary": "Art questions the reduced school schedule (2.5 days) with morning gymnastics, asks 'are you and Weston really sure you can run a school with twins?' and says '4 days a week of individual instruction was better than 5 of a classroom. More kids and less time is a lot less education.' Julie explains the schedule, says it's the same gym hours, and that fluff time is being cut not academics. Art says 'As long as it's the fluff getting cut out... Not the academics.' Julie says the second grade curriculum looks too easy for O and she'll buy 3rd grade materials too.", "relevance": "Directly relevant to fighting about school: Art and Julie disagree over the reduced school schedule and whether it provides adequate education, with Art expressing concern about less school time and Julie defending the arrangement.", "disposition": "used", "rationale": "June 26, 2024 disagreement over reduced school schedule: Art questions 2.5 days of school, says '4 days a week of individual instruction was better than 5 of a classroom. More kids and less time is a lot less education.' Julie defends the arrangement."}, {"range_id": "r000012", "thread_id": "julie_kramer", "start_message_id": "decipher_export_19:788", "end_message_id": "decipher_export_19:793", "summary": "Art says Olivia 'needs to learn to exist and participate in groups in a safe and fun way' and calls it 'one of my biggest worries with her in not real school.' Julie responds that 120 kids at theater camp is 'a big jump' from what Olivia is used to. They discuss whether Olivia should attend a theater arts camp that conflicts with morning gymnastics. Julie lists concerns including missing gym, the gym party, and seeing Momo, and worries about germs before the birthday party. Art says 'She won't finish anything in three days' and 'I really thought we had this cleared a long time ago.'", "relevance": "Relevant to fighting about school: Art explicitly raises concern about Olivia being in 'not real school' and needing group participation skills, which Julie pushes back on as a big jump. This is a disagreement about educational/socialization arrangements.", "disposition": "used", "rationale": "July 10, 2024 disagreement about educational/socialization arrangements: Art explicitly raises concern about Olivia being in 'not real school' and needing group participation skills; Julie pushes back, calling 120 kids at theater camp 'a big jump.'"}, {"range_id": "r000013", "thread_id": "julie_kramer", "start_message_id": "decipher_export_19:3370", "end_message_id": "decipher_export_19:3397", "summary": "Art and Julie have a heated disagreement about Olivia's education and schoolwork. Art states Olivia should be 'OUT of gymnastics until her education is on track,' claims she did zero school for two-week stretches at his house, and says she needs regularity in lessons. Julie counters that Olivia is on track, that Art hasn't seen the hours of school done at her house, and that Olivia told Julie the farm schoolwork was 'way too easy, like pre-school stuff.'